2012 POPULATION & HOUSING CENSUS
FINAL RESULTS
May 10, 2016
Note on the Final Adjusted Population Count
During a census, it is not always possible to collect data from all households within a given Enumeration District (ED), and by
extension a region. This was the case in the 2012 Census, when 4,766 households across the ten (10) administrative regions in Guyana
were not enumerated because the enumerators encountered extreme difficulties to interview them. The first category was those who
were not at home for various unknown reasons, while the second was those who refused to be interviewed. After a series of attempts
by the enumerators failed, they enquired from the neighbours on the occupancy of those buildings or dwellings on census night.
Thereafter, they were labeled ‘No-Contact’ or generally ‘Non-responding households’ and recorded on the visitation record per
enumeration district into two categories as ‘Refusals’ and ‘No one at home’ households. The Visitation Record was an official census
form designed to capture basic information on the population within an ED during the enumeration phase of the census. It was
intended to be used as a first source for establishing the population count for an Enumeration District.
Meanwhile, the final census count has been adjusted downwards by 929 persons from the preliminary count, a difference of 0.12
percent. This difference is insignificant and within acceptable limits according to demographic standards. The detail of the adjustment
will be included in the roll out of the analysis which will be disseminated shortly starting with the National Population Trends:
National Size and Growth.
